    <!-- WHAT IS CAP RATE -->
    <section class="content-section" id="about-cap-rate">
      <h2>What Is Capitalization Rate — and Why Does It Matter?</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong>capitalization rate (cap rate)</strong> is the most widely used
        metric for quickly evaluating a real estate investment. It answers a simple
        question: <em>if you paid all cash, what annual return would this property produce?</em>
      </p>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <code>Cap Rate = Net Operating Income (NOI) A Current Property Value</code>
      </div>
      <p>
        A property generating $48,000 NOI purchased for $600,000 has a
        <strong>cap rate of 8%</strong>. In most US markets in 2026, residential
        multifamily properties trade between 4%–7%, while commercial and industrial
        assets can exceed 8%–10%.
      </p>

      <h3>Cap Rate vs. Cash-on-Cash Return</h3>
      <p>
        Cap rate ignores financing — it's an all-cash metric. <strong>Cash-on-cash
        return</strong> factors in your actual mortgage payments and measures return
        on the cash you personally invested. A deal can have a 6% cap rate and a
        10% cash-on-cash return if it's well-leveraged — or a 6% cap rate and a
        3% cash-on-cash return if financing is expensive. Always run both numbers.
      </p>

      <h3>How Interest Rates Affect Cap Rates</h3>
      <p>
        When borrowing costs rise (as they have since 2022), buyers demand higher
        cap rates to maintain a positive spread over their mortgage rate. In 2026,
        with 30-year commercial rates around 7%–8%, many markets have seen cap
        rate expansion of 1–2 percentage points from their 2021 lows.
        Use the <a href="/real-estate/cap-rate-calculator">Cap Rate Calculator</a>
        to see how a shift in asking price affects your deal's yield.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- QUICK ANALYSIS CHECKLIST -->
    <section class="content-section" id="analysis-checklist">
      <h2>Investment Property Analysis Checklist</h2>
      <p>Before making an offer, run through these five numbers:</p>
      <ol class="hub-checklist">
        <li>
          <strong>Gross Rent Multiplier (GRM).</strong>
          Divide the purchase price by annual gross rent. A GRM under 10
          is generally favorable in most US markets — quick to calculate,
          good for initial screening.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Net Operating Income (NOI).</strong>
          Gross rent minus vacancy allowance (5%–10%) minus operating expenses
          (taxes, insurance, management, maintenance). This is the number that
          feeds your cap rate calculation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cap Rate.</strong>
          Use our <a href="/real-estate/cap-rate-calculator">Cap Rate Calculator</a>
          to benchmark this deal against comparable sales in the area. A cap
          rate below the risk-free rate (US 10-year Treasury) is a red flag.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Debt Service Coverage Ratio (DSCR).</strong>
          Lenders want your NOI to be at least 1.25× your annual mortgage payments.
          Below 1.0× means the property can't cover its own debt — most commercial
          lenders won't finance it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Exit strategy.</strong>
          Model the resale at 3, 5, and 10 years using realistic cap rate
          assumptions. Small cap rate changes at exit have an outsized effect
          on your total return — especially in the current rate environment.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

